Certainly, you can use Microsoft Power Automate to push information back into a ServiceNow ticket. Power Automate offers a variety of connectors and actions that enable you to integrate with ServiceNow seamlessly. Below is a general guide on how to set this up:

Prerequisites
ServiceNow Account: Ensure you have access to ServiceNow and have necessary permissions to create and update tickets.
Power Automate Account: Microsoft account with access to Power Automate.
ServiceNow API Access: Ensure the ServiceNow instance allows API access.
Steps to Create the Flow
Log into Power Automate
Go to Power Automate and sign in with your Microsoft account.
Create a New Flow
Select "Create" from the left-hand side menu.
Choose the type of flow you want to create. For pushing information back into a ServiceNow ticket, a "Automated Flow" or "Scheduled Flow" might be appropriate.
Add a Trigger
Select a trigger to start your flow. For example, you could use a trigger like "When an HTTP request is received" if you want to initiate the flow from an external system.
You might also choose triggers like "Recurrence" to run the flow at regular intervals.
Connect to ServiceNow
Click on "New step" and search for the ServiceNow connector.
Choose an action like "Get Record" if you need to fetch details from an existing ticket, or "Update Record" to update an existing ticket.
Configure the Action
Choose the appropriate ServiceNow instance from the connection dropdown.
Fill in the necessary details like Table Name (e.g., incident for incidents or task for tasks), Record ID, and fields that you want to update.
Map Your Data
Use dynamic content to map data from previous steps of your flow to the fields in your ServiceNow action.
For example, you could pull information from an email or a form response and push it into the ServiceNow ticket fields.
Save and Test Your Flow
Save your flow and test it to make sure everything is configured correctly.
Review the run history to fix any issues or errors.
 
